  B vs LCDM: LCDM (an NFW halo per group with free mass) reproduces M_HSE by construction, so 'B vs closure' is the only usable separation; it is a test of whether B's shortfall is real, not a
  prediction contrast between two laws.  The cluster-scale baryon-fraction scaling (rho = -0.96, CFG34 H3) is a trend test, not forecastable from a per-group sigma in the committed budget.
  Hydrostatic masses usually run LOW (CFG34 README line 40), which would make B's shortfall larger; the allowance is carried symmetrically.

